  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Today will be the warmest day before the change back to fall- \n   like weather. \n\n-  Choppy lake conditions today due to westerly winds between 15 \n   and 25 mph, and locally higher speeds expected over the Divide.\n\n-  Stiff northeast winds are possible Friday night through\n   Saturday creating choppy conditions on area lakes.\n\n-  Cutoff Low brings widespread rain, with snow above 6000 feet \n   Saturday night into Sunday. \n\nThe synoptic setup for the next few days is interesting. A strong\nlow pressure system that exited Alaska on Wednesday will transit \ninto southern Northwest Territories today (Thursday). This in \nturn will help push a transient ridge east, causing lowering \nheights over our region. Additionally, this low pressure will \nbegin to deepen and the trough that develops will push Jet Stream \nenergy south producing breezy conditions today. The 'parent' low \npressure will continue east into the Canadian Prairie and push a \ncool air mass southward, while a piece of energy breaks off and \ndevelops a closed low off the coast of Oregon and Washington. This\nis a classic setup for a backdoor Canadian Cold front.\n\nWhat to expect? Breezy conditions develop today while remaining \nat seasonable temperatures. Dry conditions will combine with gusty\nsouthwest winds to produce elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions in southwest MT, also area lakes will develop choppy \nconditions during the afternoon. \n\nThe jet energy will provide early evening lift and produce light\nshowers or a few nighttime thunderstorms over western MT, little \nto no accumulation is expected.\n\nFriday, the backdoor cold front will begin to produce a\nnortheasterly gradient and winds slowly swing northeasterly from\nthe Glacier region, eventually south and west. Expect \ntemperatures Friday to be 10 degrees or so cooler than Thursday. \nAlso, showers and thunderstorms are possible along the front. \n\nThe closed low will push onshore Saturday and move along a line \ngenerally from the Camas Prairie ID through western MT. This is \nalso the general location of highest precipitation and lowest snow\nlevels. Snow in September means look quickly to the peaks, \nbecause what little falls will melt off in the next few days. That\nsaid, those with back country plans Saturday evening into Sunday \nshould prepare for wet and raw conditions with potentially a few \nto 6 inches of snow accumulation in the highest peaks, mainly \nnorth of I-90.",
